      "native_background_wait_ms": 0,
      "process_ms": 21342,
      "prompt": "Can you explain how a bicycle stays upright when it's moving? I've never really understood it.",
      "response": "A moving bicycle stays upright mainly because **it can steer its wheels back underneath you when you start to tip**.\n\nImagine balancing a broom upright on your palm. If it leans left, you move your hand left to get underneath it again. On a bike, steering does much the same job: if you begin falling left, a slight turn left moves the wheels back under your weight.\n\nTwo things help that happen:\n- **You make tiny steering corrections**, often without noticing.\n- **The bike’s shape helps.** The geometry of the front fork and the distribution of weight can make the front wheel steer toward a lean, helping the bike correct itself.\n\nMoving forward is what makes steering useful: turning the handlebars changes where the wheels go. At a standstill, turning them barely moves your support sideways, so balancing becomes much harder.\n\nYou may have heard that spinning wheels act like gyroscopes. They do contribute, but **that isn’t the main explanation, and spinning-wheel gyroscopic effects aren’t necessary for a bicycle to balance**.\n\nSo it’s less “motion stops you falling” and more “motion lets you keep catching yourself.”",
      "response_ms": 21342,
      "returncode": 0,
      "scored": false,
      "session": 1,
      "timed_out": false
    },
    {
      "cli_diagnostic": "",
      "daemon_background_wait_ms": 29807,
      "first_native_text_ms": 10321,
