        "timed_out": false
      },
      "native_background_wait_ms": 0,
      "process_ms": 18520,
      "prompt": "So is it mostly the gyroscopic effect, or something else?",
      "response": "**Something else: steering into a lean is the key corrective action.** If the bike starts tipping left, steering left brings the wheels back underneath you. The gyroscopic effect can help produce that steering, but it isn’t what simply “holds the bike upright.” ([news.cornell.edu](https://news.cornell.edu/stories/2011/04/researchers-explain-why-bicycles-balance-themselves?utm_source=openai))\n\nThe striking test: researchers built a bicycle with its gyroscopic effects cancelled by counter-rotating wheels—and it could still balance itself while rolling without a rider. So gyroscopic effects can contribute, but they’re **not essential**; the bike’s geometry and mass distribution also matter. ([eurekalert.org](https://www.eurekalert.org/news-releases/908183?utm_source=openai))",
      "response_ms": 18520,
      "returncode": 0,
      "scored": false,
      "session": 1,
      "timed_out": false
    },
    {
      "cli_diagnostic": "",
