      },
      "native_background_wait_ms": 0,
      "process_ms": 21353,
      "prompt": "Why is it so much harder to balance when you go very slowly?",
      "response": "Because **at low speed, steering is less effective at catching a lean**. You turn the handlebars, but the bike travels only a short distance—so the wheels don’t move sideways underneath you as quickly. You need a much bigger steering adjustment to get the same correction in time. ([www3.eng.cam.ac.uk](https://www3.eng.cam.ac.uk/~hemh1/gyrobike.htm?utm_source=openai))\n\nThat’s why crawling along involves conspicuous left-right handlebar movements, whereas at an ordinary riding speed, small corrections can do the job. ([annex.exploratorium.edu](https://annex.exploratorium.edu/cycling/brakes2.html?utm_source=openai))\n\nThink back to balancing a broom on your palm: now imagine you’re only allowed to move your hand very slowly. You can still catch a tiny lean, but once it gets away from you, catching up becomes difficult.",
      "response_ms": 21353,
